Lab: Managing and Automating System Patching
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Configure automated system patching schedules using DNF automatic and systemd timers
Automate patching across multiple Linux systems using Ansible
Validate patching operations through system logs and generated reports
Troubleshoot common patching issues in an automated patch management pipeline
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux system administration
Familiarity with command-line interface operations
Knowledge of package management systems (dnf/yum)
Basic knowledge of shell scripting
Familiarity with systemd services and timers
This lab is designed to run on a single CentOS/RHEL 8 (or compatible, e.g. Rocky Linux 8 / AlmaLinux 8) machine acting as both the management node and the target of local Ansible runs. All work is performed inside your own isolated practice environment.

Install the required tools before starting:

sudo dnf install -y ansible dnf-automatic bc
Verify the installation:

ansible --version
rpm -q dnf-automatic
Expected output: ansible prints a version string (2.9 or later), and rpm -q dnf-automatic returns a package name/version line rather than "package is not installed."

Task 1: Configure Automated Patching Schedules
Subtask 1.1: Configure DNF Automatic
Back up and edit the DNF automatic configuration:
sudo cp /etc/dnf/automatic.conf /etc/dnf/automatic.conf.bak
sudo nano /etc/dnf/automatic.conf
Set the following key values in the [commands] and [emitters] sections (leave the rest of the file at defaults):
[commands]
upgrade_type = default
random_sleep = 0
network_online_timeout = 60
download_updates = yes
apply_updates = yes

[emitters]
emit_via = stdio

[base]
debuglevel = 1
Enable and start the timer:
sudo systemctl enable --now dnf-automatic.timer
systemctl status dnf-automatic.timer --no-pager
systemctl list-timers dnf-automatic.timer --no-pager
Deliverable: systemctl list-timers dnf-automatic.timer shows a row with a populated NEXT and LEFT column for dnf-automatic.timer, confirming the timer is scheduled. systemctl status dnf-automatic.timer reports Active: active (waiting).

Subtask 1.2: Create a Custom Weekly Security-Update Timer
Create the service unit:
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/security-updates.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Security Updates Service
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/security-update.sh
User=root
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
EOF
Create the timer unit:
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/security-updates.timer > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Weekly Security Updates Timer
Requires=security-updates.service

[Timer]
OnCalendar=Sun 02:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=1800

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
EOF
Create the security update script:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/security-update.sh > /dev/null << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

LOG_FILE="/var/log/security-updates.log"
DATE=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')

echo "[$DATE] Starting security updates..." >> "$LOG_FILE"

dnf --security update -y >> "$LOG_FILE" 2>&1

if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "[$DATE] Security updates completed successfully" >> "$LOG_FILE"
else
    echo "[$DATE] Security updates failed" >> "$LOG_FILE"
fi

echo "[$DATE] Security update process finished" >> "$LOG_FILE"
EOF
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/security-update.sh
Enable the timer and confirm it is scheduled:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now security-updates.timer
systemctl list-timers security-updates.timer --no-pager
Deliverable: systemctl list-timers security-updates.timer shows the timer with a NEXT run scheduled for the upcoming Sunday at 02:00 (plus up to 30 minutes of randomized delay). Run sudo systemctl start security-updates.service manually once to trigger the script immediately, then confirm log entries exist:

sudo systemctl start security-updates.service
cat /var/log/security-updates.log
Expected output: two or more timestamped lines showing "Starting security updates..." and "...process finished".

Task 2: Automate Patching with Ansible
Subtask 2.1: Configure Ansible for Local Management
Create the Ansible working directory and inventory:
mkdir -p ~/patch-lab/playbooks
cat > ~/patch-lab/inventory.ini << 'EOF'
[patch_targets]
localhost ansible_connection=local
EOF
Verify Ansible can reach the target:
ansible -i ~/patch-lab/inventory.ini patch_targets -m ping
Expected output: localhost | SUCCESS => { "changed": false, "ping": "pong" }.

Note: This lab uses localhost with a local connection to keep the environment self-contained. In a real deployment, you would replace this with the actual hostnames or IP addresses of remote systems and use SSH key-based authentication (ansible_user, ansible_ssh_private_key_file) instead of a local connection.

Subtask 2.2: Create a Patch Management Playbook
Create the playbook file:
cat > ~/patch-lab/playbooks/system-patching.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Automated System Patching
  hosts: patch_targets
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes

  vars:
    patch_log_dir: "/var/log/ansible-patching"

  pre_tasks:
    - name: Create patch log directory
      file:
        path: "{{ patch_log_dir }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Record pre-patch system state
      shell: |
        echo "=== Pre-Patch System State ===" > {{ patch_log_dir }}/pre-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log
        uname -a >> {{ patch_log_dir }}/pre-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log
        df -h >> {{ patch_log_dir }}/pre-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log
      args:
        creates: "{{ patch_log_dir }}/pre-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log"

  tasks:
    - name: Check available security updates
      command: dnf --security check-update
      register: security_check
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: security_check.rc not in [0, 100]

    - name: Apply security updates
      dnf:
        name: "*"
        state: latest
        security: yes
      register: security_updates

    - name: Check if a reboot is required
      shell: |
        needs-restarting -r
      register: reboot_check
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: reboot_check.rc not in [0, 1]

  post_tasks:
    - name: Record post-patch system state
      shell: |
        echo "=== Post-Patch System State ===" > {{ patch_log_dir }}/post-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log
        uname -a >> {{ patch_log_dir }}/post-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log
        df -h >> {{ patch_log_dir }}/post-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log

    - name: Display patch summary
      debug:
        msg: "Security updates changed: {{ security_updates.changed }}, reboot required: {{ reboot_check.rc == 1 }}"
EOF
Note: needs-restarting -r is provided by the dnf-utils (yum-utils) package; install it with sudo dnf install -y dnf-utils if it is not already present. It returns exit code 1 when a reboot is required and 0 otherwise.

Run the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i ~/patch-lab/inventory.ini ~/patch-lab/playbooks/system-patching.yml
Deliverable: The play completes with a PLAY RECAP line showing localhost with failed=0. The debug task prints a message showing whether updates changed the system and whether a reboot is required. Confirm the log files were created:

sudo ls -la /var/log/ansible-patching/
Expected output: a pre-patch-<epoch>.log and post-patch-<epoch>.log file pair, each containing uname -a and df -h output.

Task 3: Validate Patching with Logs and Reports
Subtask 3.1: Create a Validation Script
Create the validation script:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/validate-patching.sh > /dev/null << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

VALIDATION_LOG="/var/log/patch-validation.log"
DATE=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
FAILED_CHECKS=0

log_result() {
    echo "[$DATE] $1" | tee -a "$VALIDATION_LOG"
}

check_disk_space() {
    log_result "=== Disk Space Check ==="
    disk_usage=$(df / | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | sed 's/%//')
    if [ "$disk_usage" -gt 90 ]; then
        log_result "WARNING: High disk usage: ${disk_usage}%"
        FAILED_CHECKS=$((FAILED_CHECKS + 1))
    else
        log_result "OK: Disk usage normal: ${disk_usage}%"
    fi
}

check_services() {
    log_result "=== Service Status Check ==="
    critical_services=("sshd" "systemd-logind")

    for service in "${critical_services[@]}"; do
        if systemctl is-active --quiet "$service"; then
            log_result "OK: $service is running"
        else
            log_result "ERROR: $service is not running"
            FAILED_CHECKS=$((FAILED_CHECKS + 1))
        fi
    done
}

check_security_updates() {
    log_result "=== Security Updates Check ==="
    dnf --security check-update > /tmp/sec-check.out 2>&1
    rc=$?
    if [ $rc -eq 100 ]; then
        pending=$(grep -c '^[a-zA-Z0-9]' /tmp/sec-check.out || true)
        log_result "WARNING: security updates appear available (exit code 100)"
        FAILED_CHECKS=$((FAILED_CHECKS + 1))
    elif [ $rc -eq 0 ]; then
        log_result "OK: No pending security updates"
    else
        log_result "ERROR: dnf check-update failed with exit code $rc"
        FAILED_CHECKS=$((FAILED_CHECKS + 1))
    fi
}

generate_summary() {
    log_result "=== Validation Summary ==="
    log_result "Total failed checks: $FAILED_CHECKS"
    if [ "$FAILED_CHECKS" -eq 0 ]; then
        log_result "RESULT: All validations passed"
    else
        log_result "RESULT: $FAILED_CHECKS validation(s) failed"
    fi
}

log_result "Starting post-patch validation"
check_disk_space
check_services
check_security_updates
generate_summary

exit $FAILED_CHECKS
EOF
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/validate-patching.sh
Run the validation script:
sudo /usr/local/bin/validate-patching.sh
echo "Exit code: $?"
Deliverable: The console and /var/log/patch-validation.log show four sections (Disk Space Check, Service Status Check, Security Updates Check, Validation Summary) each ending in OK, WARNING, or ERROR lines, followed by a RESULT line. The exit code equals the number of failed checks reported in the summary (0 if everything passed).

Subtask 3.2: Generate a Patch Report
Create the reporting script:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/generate-patch-report.sh > /dev/null << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

REPORT_DIR="/var/reports/patching"
DATE=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d')
REPORT_FILE="$REPORT_DIR/patch-report-$DATE.html"

mkdir -p "$REPORT_DIR"

TOTAL_PACKAGES=$(rpm -qa | wc -l)
SECURITY_OUTPUT=$(dnf --security check-update 2>&1)
SECURITY_RC=$?
if [ $SECURITY_RC -eq 100 ]; then
    SECURITY_STATUS="Updates available"
elif [ $SECURITY_RC -eq 0 ]; then
    SECURITY_STATUS="No pending security updates"
else
    SECURITY_STATUS="Check failed (exit code $SECURITY_RC)"
fi

cat > "$REPORT_FILE" << HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>System Patching Report - $DATE</title>
    <style>
        body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 20px; }
        .header { background-color: #2c3e50; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 5px; }
        .section { margin: 20px 0; padding: 15px; border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 5px; }
        table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0; }
        th, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }
        th { background-color: #f2f2f2; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="header">
        <h1>System Patching Report</h1>
        <p>Generated on: $(date)</p>
        <p>Hostname: $(hostname)</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section">
        <h2>System Information</h2>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Kernel Version</td><td>$(uname -r)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Architecture</td><td>$(uname -m)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>$(uptime -p)</td></tr>
        </table>
    </div>
    <div class="section">
        <h2>Patch Statistics</h2>
        <p>Total installed packages: $TOTAL_PACKAGES</p>
        <p>Security update status: $SECURITY_STATUS</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section">
        <h2>Latest Validation Log</h2>
        <pre>
$(tail -30 /var/log/patch-validation.log 2>/dev/null || echo "No validation log available yet.")
        </pre>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
HTML

echo "Report generated: $REPORT_FILE"
EOF
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/generate-patch-report.sh
Run the reporting script and inspect the result:
sudo /usr/local/bin/generate-patch-report.sh
cat /var/reports/patching/patch-report-$(date '+%Y-%m-%d').html | head -20
Deliverable: The script prints Report generated: /var/reports/patching/patch-report-<date>.html, and the file exists containing valid HTML with populated System Information, Patch Statistics, and Latest Validation Log sections.

Subtask 3.3: Run and Verify the Complete Workflow
Execute the full workflow end to end in sequence:
sudo /usr/local/bin/validate-patching.sh
VALIDATION_RC=$?

ansible-playbook -i ~/patch-lab/inventory.ini ~/patch-lab/playbooks/system-patching.yml
ANSIBLE_RC=$?

sudo /usr/local/bin/validate-patching.sh
sudo /usr/local/bin/generate-patch-report.sh

echo "Initial validation exit code: $VALIDATION_RC"
echo "Ansible playbook exit code: $ANSIBLE_RC"
Confirm every artifact produced by the workflow exists:
sudo ls -la /var/log/ansible-patching/
sudo tail -20 /var/log/patch-validation.log
sudo ls -la /var/reports/patching/
Expected output: ANSIBLE_RC is 0, the /var/log/ansible-patching/ directory contains matching pre-patch and post-patch log pairs, /var/log/patch-validation.log contains at least two full validation runs (each ending with a RESULT line), and /var/reports/patching/ contains an HTML report dated today.

Troubleshooting this step:

If ansible-playbook fails at the "Apply security updates" task with a permission error, confirm you are running the command as a user with passwordless sudo, or add --ask-become-pass and supply the sudo password when prompted.
If dnf --security check-update always exits 0 even though updates are known to be available, confirm the system has any enabled repositories providing updates (dnf repolist); a system with no update-providing repos will always report no security updates.
Deliverable: A completed end-to-end run showing exit code 0 from the Ansible playbook, at least two validation log entries, and one HTML report file, demonstrating that scheduling, automated patch application, and validation/reporting all function together correctly.

Expected Outcomes
A working dnf-automatic.timer and custom security-updates.timer scheduled and verified with systemctl list-timers
A functioning Ansible playbook that checks for, applies, and logs security updates with pre- and post-patch system state captured
A validation script and HTML reporting script that together confirm system health and produce an auditable record of each patch cycle
Troubleshooting
Timer never fires or shows no NEXT value: Run systemctl status <timer-name> to check for enable --now errors, and confirm the system clock and timezone are correct with timedatectl, since OnCalendar schedules are time-zone dependent.
Ansible playbook fails at the dnf module task with a "Failed to synchronize cache" error: Check network/repository connectivity with dnf repolist on the target host; the dnf Ansible module requires the same repo access as the command line dnf tool.
Conclusion
In this lab, you built a small but complete automated patch management pipeline: scheduled security updates using both dnf-automatic and a custom systemd timer, automated patch application and system-state logging with an Ansible playbook, and validated the results with a health-check script and an HTML reporting tool. Together these pieces demonstrate the core enterprise patching pattern of schedule, apply, validate, and report, which is essential for maintaining security compliance while minimizing unplanned downtime. In a production environment, these same techniques would be extended with a real inventory of remote hosts, staged rollouts across environments (development before production), enforced maintenance windows, and automated rollback procedures triggered by failed validation checks.
